VALUES

-- Q1: hard / L4 — Evaluate two-class method for participating securities
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Glenmore Industries has 500,000 common shares outstanding and 50,000 shares of participating preferred stock that receives $2 per share before common shareholders, then shares equally in additional distributions. Net income is $1,200,000 and total dividends declared are $200,000. The CFO calculates basic EPS using the standard formula without allocating undistributed earnings to the preferred shares. Evaluate whether this approach is correct under ASC 260.', '["The CFO''s approach is incorrect because ASC 260-10-45-61A requires the two-class method, which allocates undistributed earnings to participating securities", "The CFO''s approach is correct because participating preferred stock is excluded from basic EPS calculations", "The CFO''s approach is incorrect because participating securities should only be included in diluted EPS", "The CFO''s approach is correct because the two-class method applies only when dividends exceed net income"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, entities with participating securities must use the two-class method to compute basic EPS. This method allocates undistributed earnings between common shares and participating securities based on their respective dividend rights. The CFO''s approach is incorrect because it ignores the preferred stock''s participation right in undistributed earnings. Choice B is wrong because participating preferred stock is explicitly included in basic EPS via the two-class method.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q2: easy / L1 — Definition of complex capital structure
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best describes a complex capital structure?', '["A structure with only common stock and retained earnings", "A structure with more than one class of common stock but no convertible securities", "A structure where all debt is classified as long-term", "A structure that includes securities potentially convertible into common stock"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, a complex capital structure exists when an entity has outstanding securities that could potentially dilute EPS, such as convertible bonds, convertible preferred stock, stock options, or warrants. A simple capital structure has only common stock outstanding with no dilutive potential. Choice A describes a simple capital structure, not a complex one.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q3: hard / L4 — Evaluate mid-year convertible preferred and dilutiveness
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'On July 1, Briarwood Holdings issues 10,000 shares of $50 par, 8% convertible preferred stock, each share convertible into 5 common shares. Net income for the year is $3,000,000 with 600,000 weighted-average common shares. Basic EPS is $4.93 (after deducting $20,000 preferred dividends for six months). The controller tests diluted EPS by adding back the full $40,000 annual dividend and 50,000 shares. Evaluate whether the controller''s if-converted calculation is correct.', '["The controller''s approach is incorrect because only the dividends actually declared should be added back", "The controller''s approach is correct because the if-converted method always assumes conversion at the beginning of the year", "The controller''s approach is incorrect because both the dividend add-back and incremental shares should reflect only the period from July 1 to year-end", "The controller''s approach is correct because preferred stock issued mid-year is treated as outstanding for the full year"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, when convertible preferred stock is issued during the period, the if-converted method assumes conversion at the issuance date, not the beginning of the year. Both the dividend add-back ($20,000 for six months) and incremental shares (25,000 weighted-average) should reflect only the period outstanding. Choice B is wrong because full-year treatment applies only to securities outstanding at the beginning of the period.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q4: medium / L3 — Analyze effect of share buyback on basic EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Redstone Corp. has 400,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2026, and repurchases 60,000 shares on September 1, 2026, at $45 per share. Net income is $1,500,000 with no preferred stock. The investor relations team notes that the share repurchase increased basic EPS. What is the most likely effect of the repurchase on basic EPS compared to no repurchase?', '["Basic EPS decreased because the cash outflow reduced net income", "Basic EPS increased because the weighted-average shares decreased while net income remained unchanged", "Basic EPS remained the same because treasury stock has no effect on the EPS calculation", "Basic EPS increased because the repurchase price is added to the numerator"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, treasury stock repurchases reduce weighted-average shares outstanding for the period they are not outstanding. The 60,000 shares repurchased September 1 reduce weighted-average shares by 20,000 (60,000 x 4/12), lowering the denominator from 400,000 to 380,000 and increasing basic EPS from $3.75 to $3.95. Choice A is wrong because the cash payment for treasury stock does not reduce net income; it is a balance sheet equity transaction.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q5: easy / L1 — ASC 260 scope: which entities present EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which entities are required to present earnings per share on the face of the income statement?', '["All entities that prepare financial statements under U.S. GAAP", "Only entities with annual revenues exceeding $10 million", "Only entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock", "Only entities that have both common and preferred stock outstanding"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-15-2, EPS presentation is required only for entities with publicly traded common stock or potential common stock (such as options or convertible securities). Private companies are not required to present EPS, though they may do so voluntarily. Choice A is wrong because private entities preparing GAAP statements are not required to present EPS.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q6: medium / L3 — Analyze retroactive adjustment for post-period stock split
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Fernwood Manufacturing reported basic EPS of $3.20 for the year ended December 31, 2025, based on 250,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $800,000. On February 15, 2026, before the financial statements are issued, the company declares a 2-for-1 stock split. What is the most likely effect on the 2025 EPS reported in the financial statements?', '["EPS remains $3.20 because the split occurred after the reporting period", "EPS is restated to $6.40 because the split doubles the numerator", "EPS is disclosed in a footnote at $1.60 but reported on the income statement at $3.20", "EPS is restated to $1.60 because stock splits after the balance sheet date but before issuance require retroactive adjustment of EPS"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock splits or stock dividends occurring after the balance sheet date but before financial statements are issued must be given retroactive effect in computing EPS. The weighted-average shares are adjusted to 500,000 (250,000 x 2), producing EPS of $1.60. Choice A is wrong because ASC 260 specifically requires retroactive adjustment for post-period splits to ensure comparability.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q7: medium / L2 — Calculate weighted-average shares with treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Whitfield Enterprises has 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On April 1, the company repurchases 36,000 shares as treasury stock. On October 1, the company reissues 12,000 of those treasury shares. What is the weighted-average number of common shares outstanding for 2026?', '["276,000", "279,000", "282,000", "288,000"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, weighted-average shares reflect changes from the dates they occur. Jan-Mar: 300,000 for 3 months; Apr-Sep: 264,000 for 6 months; Oct-Dec: 276,000 for 3 months. Weighted-average = (300,000 x 3 + 264,000 x 6 + 276,000 x 3) / 12 = (900,000 + 1,584,000 + 828,000) / 12 = 276,000. Choice C (282,000) incorrectly weights the reissuance for six months instead of three.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q8: medium / L2 — Calculate diluted EPS with convertible bonds issued mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Kensington Corp. has net income of $960,000 and 200,000 weighted-average common shares with no preferred stock. On July 1, the company issued $1,000,000 face value of 6% convertible bonds, each $1,000 bond convertible into 50 common shares. The tax rate is 25%. What is diluted EPS?', '["$4.02", "$3.93", "$4.37", "$4.40"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible bonds issued mid-year use the if-converted method from the issuance date. Interest saved: $1,000,000 x 6% x 6/12 = $30,000; tax: $30,000 x 25% = $7,500; after-tax add-back: $22,500. Incremental shares: 1,000 bonds x 50 shares x 6/12 = 25,000. Diluted EPS = ($960,000 + $22,500) / (200,000 + 25,000) = $982,500 / 225,000 = $4.37. Choice A ($4.02) incorrectly uses full-year interest and full-year shares instead of the six-month period.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q9: medium / L2 — Calculate EPS from discontinued operations
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Oakmont Industries reports income from continuing operations of $720,000 and a loss from discontinued operations (net of tax) of $180,000. The company has 200,000 weighted-average common shares and no preferred stock. What is basic EPS from continuing operations and net income, respectively?', '["$3.60 and $2.70", "$2.70 and $3.60", "$3.60 and $3.60", "$2.70 and $2.70"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-3, per-share amounts must be presented for income from continuing operations and net income on the face of the income statement. EPS from continuing operations = $720,000 / 200,000 = $3.60. Net income = $720,000 - $180,000 = $540,000; net income EPS = $540,000 / 200,000 = $2.70. Choice B reverses the two figures, confusing continuing operations with net income.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q10: hard / L4 — Evaluate control number concept (loss from continuing operations)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Sterling Industries reports a loss from continuing operations of $400,000 and a gain on discontinued operations (net of tax) of $650,000, producing net income of $250,000. The company has 100,000 common shares and 5,000 dilutive stock options (incremental shares under treasury stock method: 2,000). The CFO includes the 2,000 incremental shares in diluted EPS for net income but excludes them from the discontinued operations line. Evaluate the CFO''s treatment.', '["The treatment is correct because dilutive securities are tested against net income, not continuing operations", "The treatment is incorrect because the options should be excluded from all per-share calculations since the control number shows a loss from continuing operations", "The treatment is correct because the options reduce the per-share loss on continuing operations", "The treatment is incorrect because dilutive securities should be excluded only from the discontinued operations line"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19, the control number for determining whether potential common shares are dilutive is income from continuing operations (adjusted for preferred dividends). Because Sterling has a loss from continuing operations, all potentially dilutive securities are treated as antidilutive and excluded from all per-share calculations, even though net income is positive. Choice A is wrong because ASC 260 explicitly uses continuing operations, not net income, as the control number.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q11: medium / L2 — Determine treatment of contingently issuable shares (condition not met)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Halcyon Corp. has 150,000 common shares outstanding and net income of $600,000. The company has 20,000 contingently issuable shares that vest if cumulative revenue exceeds $50 million by December 31, 2026. As of December 31, 2026, cumulative revenue is $42 million. How should the contingent shares be treated for basic and diluted EPS?', '["Include in both basic and diluted EPS", "Include in basic EPS only because they represent a binding commitment", "Exclude from both basic and diluted EPS because the condition was not satisfied at year-end", "Exclude from basic EPS; include in diluted EPS if the revenue condition would have been met at period-end"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48 through 51, contingently issuable shares are included in basic EPS only if all necessary conditions have been satisfied by the end of the reporting period. For diluted EPS, contingently issuable shares are included only if the conditions would have been met assuming the end of the reporting period is the end of the contingency period. Since $42 million does not meet the $50 million threshold, the shares are excluded from both. Choice D is wrong because the revenue condition was not met at period-end under either test.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q12: medium / L2 — Calculate incremental shares from written put option
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Crestview Holdings has 400,000 weighted-average shares and net income of $1,600,000. The company has written put options obligating it to repurchase 20,000 shares at $60 per share. The average market price during the period is $40 per share. Using the reverse treasury stock method, how many incremental shares are added to the denominator for diluted EPS?', '["10,000", "20,000", "30,000", "0"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, written put options are dilutive when the exercise price exceeds the average market price. Using the reverse treasury stock method, the entity assumes it issues enough shares at market price to fund the repurchase: (20,000 x $60) / $40 = 30,000 shares issued. Incremental shares = 30,000 issued - 20,000 repurchased = 10,000 net shares added. Choice B incorrectly uses the number of put shares without applying the reverse treasury stock method.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q13: medium / L3 — Analyze why convertible bonds are antidilutive
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Pinnacle Systems has basic EPS of $5.00 based on $1,000,000 net income and 200,000 shares. The company has $200,000 of 5% convertible bonds (each $1,000 bond converts into 50 shares, tax rate 25%). After applying the if-converted method, diluted EPS calculates to $5.04. What is the most likely conclusion?', '["The convertible bonds are dilutive and should be included, producing diluted EPS of $5.04", "The convertible bonds are antidilutive and should be excluded, so diluted EPS equals basic EPS of $5.00", "The calculation contains an error because diluted EPS must always be lower than basic EPS", "The convertible bonds should be partially included to bring diluted EPS to exactly $5.00"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-17, potential common shares are antidilutive when their inclusion increases EPS or decreases loss per share. Because including the convertible bonds produces $5.04, which exceeds basic EPS of $5.00, they are antidilutive and must be excluded. Diluted EPS is reported as $5.00, equal to basic EPS. Choice C is incorrect because diluted EPS can equal basic EPS when all potential shares are antidilutive; it simply cannot exceed it.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q14: medium / L2 — Calculate weighted-average shares for acquisition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Meridian Corp. has 300,000 common shares outstanding on January 1, 2026. On September 1, 2026, Meridian acquires Beacon LLC and issues 60,000 new common shares as consideration. What is the weighted-average number of common shares for the 2026 EPS calculation?', '["340,000", "360,000", "330,000", "320,000"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, shares issued for an acquisition are included in the weighted-average from the acquisition date. Weighted-average = 300,000 x (8/12) + 360,000 x (4/12) = 200,000 + 120,000 = 320,000 shares. Choice A (340,000) incorrectly weights the new shares from July 1 (six months) rather than from the September 1 acquisition date.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q15: easy / L1 — Definition of antidilutive security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, what does the term "antidilutive" mean in the context of earnings per share?', '["A security that reduces the number of shares outstanding", "A security whose inclusion in the EPS calculation would increase EPS or decrease loss per share", "A security that has been converted into common stock during the period", "A security that reduces net income available to common shareholders"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, antidilutive refers to an increase in EPS or a decrease in loss per share that would result from including a potential common share in the diluted EPS calculation. Antidilutive securities are excluded from diluted EPS because their inclusion would make the per-share result more favorable, not less. Choice D is wrong because reducing net income relates to dilutive effects on the numerator, not the antidilutive concept.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q16: medium / L3 — Analyze effect of mid-year option grant on diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Lakeview Corp. grants 30,000 stock options on August 1, 2026, with an exercise price of $20. The average market price from August 1 through December 31 is $50 per share. For the full-year diluted EPS calculation, the treasury stock method yields 18,000 incremental shares for the August-December period. How should the 18,000 incremental shares be reflected in the full-year diluted EPS denominator?', '["Include 18,000 shares for the full year", "Include 30,000 shares weighted for 5 out of 12 months", "Include 18,000 shares, but only if the options are exercised before year-end", "Include 7,500 shares, weighted for 5 out of 12 months"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-29, dilutive securities outstanding for only part of the period are weighted for the time they are outstanding. The 18,000 incremental shares from the treasury stock method apply to the August-December period (5 months) and must be time-weighted: 18,000 x 5/12 = 7,500 incremental shares for the annual denominator. Choice A is wrong because including the full 18,000 would overstate dilution by ignoring the partial-period weighting.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q17: easy / L1 — Required dual presentation (basic and diluted)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, an entity with a complex capital structure must present which EPS figures on the face of the income statement?', '["Only basic EPS", "Only diluted EPS", "Both basic EPS and diluted EPS with equal prominence", "Basic EPS on the income statement and diluted EPS in the footnotes"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, entities with complex capital structures must present both basic and diluted EPS with equal prominence on the face of the income statement. Neither measure may be relegated to footnote disclosure. Choice A is wrong because presenting only basic EPS would omit the dilutive effects of outstanding convertible securities, options, and other potential common shares.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q18: easy / L1 — Treasury stock method definition
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which method is used to calculate the dilutive effect of stock options and warrants on earnings per share?', '["The if-converted method", "The weighted-average method", "The book value method", "The treasury stock method"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-22, the treasury stock method is used to determine the dilutive impact of stock options and warrants. This method assumes the options are exercised at the beginning of the period and that the proceeds are used to repurchase common shares at the average market price. The if-converted method (choice A) applies to convertible debt and convertible preferred stock, not to options or warrants.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q19: hard / L4 — Evaluate mandatorily redeemable preferred stock in EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Stratton Financial issues 10,000 shares of mandatorily redeemable preferred stock with a $100 par value and 7% dividend rate. Under ASC 480, this instrument is classified as a liability. Net income is $2,500,000 with 500,000 weighted-average common shares. The treasurer computes basic EPS by deducting the $70,000 preferred dividend from the numerator, treating it like traditional preferred stock. Evaluate this approach.', '["The approach is correct because all preferred dividends reduce the EPS numerator regardless of classification", "The approach is incorrect because the dividends on mandatorily redeemable preferred classified as a liability are recognized as interest expense and already reduce net income", "The approach is correct because mandatorily redeemable preferred stock is always treated as equity for EPS purposes", "The approach is incorrect because mandatorily redeemable preferred dividends should only be deducted in diluted EPS"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 480-10-25-4 and ASC 260-10-45-11A, mandatorily redeemable preferred stock classified as a liability under ASC 480 has its dividends treated as interest expense in the income statement. Since these amounts already reduce net income, deducting them again from the numerator would double-count the charge. Choice A is wrong because the liability classification changes how distributions flow through the income statement, eliminating the need for a separate numerator adjustment.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q20: easy / L2 — Calculate basic EPS with mid-year treasury stock repurchase
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Bellwood Industries has 200,000 shares outstanding on January 1, 2026, and repurchases 24,000 shares on July 1, 2026. Net income is $528,000 with no preferred dividends. What is basic EPS for 2026?', '["$2.81", "$2.64", "$3.00", "$2.40"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-10, the weighted-average shares account for the treasury stock repurchase from the repurchase date. Weighted-average = 200,000 x (6/12) + 176,000 x (6/12) = 100,000 + 88,000 = 188,000 shares. Basic EPS = $528,000 / 188,000 = $2.81. Choice B ($2.64) incorrectly uses the full 200,000 shares for the entire year, ignoring the repurchase effect.', 'easy', 2),

-- Q21: hard / L3 — Analyze interim EPS reporting requirements
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Harborview Corp. reports quarterly financial data. In Q1, the company had a loss from continuing operations of $100,000 and net income of $50,000 (due to a discontinued operations gain). In Q2, income from continuing operations was $300,000. The company has 100,000 shares and dilutive stock options yielding 5,000 incremental shares. Analyze how the dilutive securities should be treated in Q1 versus Q2 interim EPS.', '["Include dilutive shares in both quarters because full-year net income is expected to be positive", "Include dilutive shares in Q1 based on net income being positive; exclude in Q2 if year-to-date EPS would be antidilutive", "Exclude dilutive shares from both quarters and test dilution only at year-end", "Exclude dilutive shares in Q1 because the control number (loss from continuing operations) is negative; include them in Q2 where continuing operations income is positive"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-19 and ASC 270-10-45, interim EPS calculations use the same control number principle: income or loss from continuing operations determines dilutiveness for each interim period independently. In Q1, the loss from continuing operations makes all potentially dilutive securities antidilutive, regardless of net income. In Q2, with positive continuing operations income, the options are included. Choice A is wrong because each interim period is evaluated independently using its own control number.', 'hard', 3),

-- Q22: medium / L2 — Determine contingent shares treatment (performance vesting)
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Northgate Corp. has 250,000 shares outstanding and net income of $875,000. The company granted 15,000 performance shares to executives on January 1, 2026, that vest on December 31, 2028, if cumulative EPS exceeds $10.00 over the three-year period. At December 31, 2026, cumulative EPS is $3.50. How are the performance shares treated in the 2026 diluted EPS calculation?', '["Included because the shares will likely vest based on projected future earnings", "Included at 5,000 shares (one-third of the total for year one)", "Excluded because the contingency condition is not yet satisfied at the reporting date", "Excluded from diluted EPS but included in basic EPS"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-48, contingently issuable shares are included in diluted EPS only if the contingency conditions would be satisfied assuming the reporting date is the end of the contingency period. At December 31, 2026, cumulative EPS of $3.50 does not meet the $10.00 threshold, so the shares are excluded from diluted EPS. Choice A is wrong because projected future earnings cannot be used to satisfy the condition; only current-period results are considered.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q23: medium / L3 — Analyze forward purchase contracts in diluted EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Westbrook Inc. enters a forward contract obligating it to repurchase 25,000 of its own shares at $55 per share in 18 months. The average market price during the current reporting period is $40 per share. Analyze how this forward contract affects diluted EPS under ASC 260.', '["The contract is dilutive and adds incremental shares to the denominator because the repurchase price exceeds the average market price", "The contract has no effect on diluted EPS because it settles in a future period", "The contract is antidilutive and should be excluded because the repurchase price is below the average market price", "The contract is dilutive but only affects the numerator, not the denominator"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-35, forward purchase contracts to repurchase an entity''s own shares are treated similarly to written put options. When the repurchase price ($55) exceeds the average market price ($40), the contract is dilutive. Using the reverse treasury stock method: (25,000 x $55) / $40 = 34,375 shares issued; net incremental = 34,375 - 25,000 = 9,375 shares added. Choice C reverses the dilutiveness test by incorrectly comparing prices.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q24: medium / L2 — Calculate numerator/denominator adjustments for convertible preferred issued mid-year
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Ashbury Corp. has net income of $1,200,000 and 400,000 weighted-average common shares for 2026. On April 1, 2026, the company issues 5,000 shares of $100 par, 10% convertible preferred stock, each share convertible into 8 common shares. What are the numerator and denominator adjustments for the if-converted method in computing diluted EPS?', '["Add back $50,000 preferred dividends; add 40,000 incremental shares", "Add back $37,500 preferred dividends; add 30,000 incremental shares", "Add back $50,000 preferred dividends; add 30,000 incremental shares", "Add back $37,500 preferred dividends; add 40,000 incremental shares"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-40, convertible preferred stock issued during the period assumes conversion at the issuance date. From April 1 to December 31 is 9 months: dividend add-back = 5,000 x $100 x 10% x 9/12 = $37,500. Incremental shares = 5,000 x 8 x 9/12 = 30,000. Both the numerator and denominator reflect only the 9 months outstanding. Choice A incorrectly uses a full year for both calculations, overstating the adjustments.', 'medium', 2),

-- Q25: hard / L4 — Evaluate retrospective EPS adjustment after error correction
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'During 2026, Clayborne Inc. discovers a material error in its 2024 financial statements that understated net income by $240,000. The company had 300,000 weighted-average shares in 2024 and originally reported basic EPS of $4.00 (based on $1,200,000 net income). The controller plans to restate 2024 EPS in the comparative financial statements. Evaluate the proper restated 2024 basic EPS.', '["$4.00, because prior-period EPS is not restated for error corrections", "$3.20, because the error correction reduces the prior-period income", "$4.80, reflecting the corrected net income of $1,440,000 divided by 300,000 shares", "$4.80, but presented only in the footnotes, not on the face of the income statement"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 250-10-45-1 and ASC 260-10-45-7, when a prior-period error is corrected through restatement, EPS for all prior periods presented must be restated retroactively. Corrected net income = $1,200,000 + $240,000 = $1,440,000. Restated EPS = $1,440,000 / 300,000 = $4.80. The restated amount appears on the face of the income statement, not just in footnotes. Choice A is wrong because ASC 250 requires retroactive restatement of all affected financial data, including EPS.', 'hard', 4),

-- Q26: easy / L1 — Identify per-share amounts required on income statement
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, for which of the following must per-share amounts be presented on the face of the income statement?', '["Gross profit and operating income", "Revenue and cost of goods sold", "Total assets and total equity", "Income from continuing operations and net income"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, per-share amounts must be presented on the face of the income statement for income from continuing operations and net income. If an entity reports a discontinued operation, per-share amounts for that item must also be presented either on the face or in the notes. Choice A is wrong because gross profit and operating income do not require per-share presentation under ASC 260.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q27: medium / L3 — Analyze stock dividend declared after year-end effect on prior-year EPS
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Thornberry Corp. reports 2025 basic EPS of $6.00 based on $1,800,000 net income and 300,000 weighted-average shares. On January 20, 2026, before the 2025 financial statements are issued, the board declares a 25% stock dividend. When the company presents comparative financial statements for 2025 and 2026, what is the effect on the reported 2025 basic EPS?', '["2025 EPS is retroactively adjusted to $4.80, reflecting 375,000 adjusted weighted-average shares", "2025 EPS is adjusted to $7.50 because the stock dividend increases the numerator", "2025 EPS is presented at $6.00 with a footnote disclosing the stock dividend", "2025 EPS remains $6.00 because dividends declared in 2026 do not affect 2025"]'::jsonb, 0, 'Under ASC 260-10-55-12, stock dividends occurring after the reporting period but before financial statement issuance require retroactive adjustment of weighted-average shares for all periods presented. Adjusted shares = 300,000 x 1.25 = 375,000. Restated EPS = $1,800,000 / 375,000 = $4.80. This ensures comparability across periods. Choice D is wrong because ASC 260 explicitly requires retroactive adjustment for post-period stock dividends.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q28: medium / L3 — Analyze participating preferred allocation of undistributed earnings
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Millcroft Industries has 800,000 common shares and 100,000 participating preferred shares. Net income is $2,700,000 and declared dividends total $500,000 ($100,000 to preferred, $400,000 to common). Under the two-class method, undistributed earnings of $2,200,000 must be allocated. The preferred shares participate equally with common on a per-share basis after the initial preference. How are the undistributed earnings allocated?', '["$2,200,000 entirely to common shareholders", "$1,955,556 to common and $244,444 to participating preferred", "$2,000,000 to common and $200,000 to participating preferred", "$1,760,000 to common and $440,000 to participating preferred"]'::jsonb, 1, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-61A, the two-class method allocates undistributed earnings to each class based on participation rights. With equal per-share participation: common allocation = $2,200,000 x (800,000 / 900,000) = $1,955,556; preferred allocation = $2,200,000 x (100,000 / 900,000) = $244,444. Choice A is wrong because it ignores the preferred stock''s participation right in undistributed earnings, which the two-class method requires.', 'medium', 3),

-- Q29: easy / L1 — Definition of participating security
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under ASC 260, which of the following best describes a participating security?', '["A security that has been converted into common stock during the current period", "A security that grants voting rights to its holder", "A security that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock", "A security that is traded on a public stock exchange"]'::jsonb, 2, 'Under ASC 260-10-20, a participating security is one that may participate in undistributed earnings with common stock, whether that participation is conditional or unconditional. Examples include certain preferred stock and some restricted stock grants. Choice A is wrong because conversion into common stock describes a convertible security, not a participating security specifically.', 'easy', 1),

-- Q30: easy / L1 — EPS presentation location
(2, 'Earnings Per Share', 'Under U.S. GAAP, where must earnings per share data be presented for a publicly traded company?', '["In the footnotes to the financial statements only", "In the management discussion and analysis section", "On the face of the balance sheet", "On the face of the income statement"]'::jsonb, 3, 'Under ASC 260-10-45-2, publicly traded entities must present basic and diluted EPS on the face of the income statement (or statement of comprehensive income if presented in a single continuous statement). EPS data cannot be relegated to footnotes alone. Choice A is wrong because ASC 260 specifically requires face-of-statement presentation, though supplemental disclosures may appear in the notes.', 'easy', 1)
;

COMMIT;
